Genital Herpes
Young Adult & Adolecence
Painful grouped vesicles/ulcers, Tender inguinal lymphadenopathy, Prodromal tingling/burning

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-018
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing genital pain resulting in acute urinary retention and inability to ambulate.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a patient experiencing severe, unrelenting anogenital pain and an inability to pass urine for the past 10 hours due to excruciating dysuria and localized swelling. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly required to evaluate for acute urinary retention requiring catheterization, exclude a severe secondary bacterial cellulitis, and immediately initiate systemic antiviral and analgesic therapies prior to morning clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have these agonizing blisters everywhere down there, and I literally cannot pee because the burning is so intense."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 4-day history of an acute, severely painful genital eruption. The episode began with prodromal symptoms of localized tingling, burning, and severe pruritus. Over the next 48 hours, grouped painful vesicular lesions appeared on an erythematous base across the vulva and perineum. Many of these have since ruptured, leaving shallow, exquisitely tender ulcers. The patient reports profound systemic symptoms, including a fever, headache, and myalgia, indicating a primary infection. Over the last 12 hours, severe localized oedema and intense dysuria have led to voluntary urinary retention secondary to severe pain when urine contacts the ulcers.
Functional Status: Bedbound; assuming a wide-legged posture to prevent friction; unable to void.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is highly distressed, in severe pain, and anxious regarding the diagnosis and the acute inability to urinate.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/80 | HR: 105 | Temp: 38.3 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Patient is febrile, diaphoretic, and in obvious acute distress, shifting continuously to find a comfortable position.
Physical Exam: Genitourinary examination (chaperoned) reveals extensive, bilateral, painful grouped vesicles and shallow, unroofed ulcers on an erythematous base across the labia minora, majora, and perineum. Marked localized oedema is present. There is tender, bilateral inguinal lymphadenopathy palpable. The bladder is palpable midway to the umbilicus, dull to percussion, confirming acute urinary retention. The clinical phenotype strongly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of painful grouped vesicles/ulcers, tender inguinal lymphadenopathy, and a history of prodromal tingling/burning.
Environmental Observations: The bathroom is directly adjacent; patient has been attempting to sit in the shower to relieve pain but remains unable to void.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has taken 400mg Ibuprofen and 1000mg Paracetamol with no relief of the severe mucosal pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Primary Genital Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV) Infection with Acute Urinary Retention - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Syphilis (Primary Chancre) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of multiple, exquisitely painful, grouped vesicles and ulcers, whereas a syphilitic chancre is typically a single, painless, indurated ulcer.

Chancroid - Ruled out/unlikely due to the distinct vesicular stage preceding the ulcers and the presence of significant systemic viral symptoms (fever, myalgia), which are less common in localized chancroid.

Severe Candidiasis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of true vesicular blistering, frank ulceration, and tender lymphadenopathy, rather than isolated erythema and thick white discharge.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical Risk: High due to retention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic presentation of a primary HSV infection, complicated by severe systemic symptoms and acute, pain-induced urinary retention. The inability to void for 10 hours created an impending medical emergency requiring immediate assessment to prevent bladder overdistension and potential renal injury. Delaying care until routine daytime hours was clinically unacceptable. The urgent home visit allowed for the immediate confirmation of the diagnosis, the vital initiation of high-dose systemic antiviral therapy, and crucial interventions to facilitate micturition (topical anesthesia and warm water immersion) to avoid the need for invasive catheterization.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Valaciclovir 500mg PO stat, to be continued BID for 5-10 days. Applied 2% Lignocaine gel topically to the ulcerated labia to provide profound local anesthesia. Instructed the patient to attempt voiding while sitting in a warm bath. A viral swab of an active unroofed lesion was taken for PCR detection of HSV DNA.
Positive Behavioral Change: To facilitate painless urination and prevent severe bladder damage, apply the prescribed numbing gel 5 minutes before attempting to void, and urinate while fully submerged in a warm bath, directly diluting the acidic urine and soothing the raw tissue. To prevent auto-inoculation to other body parts (like the eyes), strictly wash your hands with soap immediately after touching the genital area, creating a hard physical barrier against viral spread.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 24 hours to review PCR swab results, confirm the resolution of urinary retention, and monitor the clinical response to antivirals.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the patient remains completely unable to pass urine despite the numbing gel and warm bath over the next 4 hours, or if severe lower abdominal/bladder pain develops.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinically highly suspected as primary genital herpes pending formal PCR swab confirmation.
Clinical Course: The systemic symptoms (fever, aches) will resolve within a few days on the antivirals, but the painful ulcers may take up to 2-3 weeks to fully heal during this first episode.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go directly to the Emergency Department if you are still completely unable to empty your bladder 4 hours from now, as you may require a temporary tube (catheter) to drain the urine and protect your kidneys.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The oral antiviral medication will stop the herpes virus from multiplying, speeding up the healing of the sores and reducing the severity of the outbreak. The topical numbing gel temporarily blocks the pain nerves so you can safely pass urine.
Actionable Steps: Coat the sores in the numbing gel and sit in a tub of warm water when you need to pee; the water dilutes the urine so it doesn't sting the open blisters. Always wash your hands thoroughly after applying the gel to avoid spreading the virus to your eyes.

